# The map that blamed a water pump
subtitle: "How John Snow traced London's 1854 cholera outbreak to one pump, and why the exceptions, not the cluster, made his case"
tags: ["epidemiology cholera outbreak","disease transmission evidence","john snow mapping"]
teaches: ["methods/epidemiological-investigation","concepts/causation-vs-correlation","history/public-health","logic/ruling-out-alternatives"]
thumb: "card purple"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Gacrux | a mature storyteller telling a Victorian detective story; measured, gripping, natural pace","take":"lines"}

## Soho, autumn 1854
use: timeline
with:
    milestones:
      - {label: 31 August, sublabel: cholera breaks out}
      - {label: Early September, sublabel: deaths listed}
      - {label: 7 September, sublabel: parish board}
      - {label: 8 September, sublabel: handle off}
      - {label: Later, sublabel: bar per death, emphasize: true}

    icon i_dead #b5482e size 110 x 330 y 575 of skull hidden true
    text t_dead "~500 dead" #b5482e x 540 y 575 font_size 44 hidden true
    icon i_air #8f887c size 110 x 230 y 575 of wind hidden true
    text t_air "bad air?" #8f887c x 390 y 575 font_size 40 hidden true
    icon i_water #2f6b8f size 110 x 620 y 575 of droplet hidden true
    text t_water "water?" #2f6b8f x 770 y 575 font_size 40 hidden true
    text t_pump "nearest one pump" #2f6b8f x 500 y 575 font_size 44 hidden true
    text t_handle "handle off" #b5482e x 500 y 575 font_size 44 hidden true
    icon i_map #8f887c size 110 x 360 y 575 of map hidden true
    text t_map "a map, later" #8f887c x 560 y 575 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "The map that blamed a water pump"

How did one map point at a single water pump, and change how we think cholera spreads? It starts on 31 August 1854, in a few streets of Soho.
    draw line dot_0 label_0

    draw i_dead t_dead parallel true

Within ten days, some five hundred people nearby were dead.
    highlight dot_0

    erase i_dead t_dead

Most doctors blamed miasma, a poisonous bad air. John Snow suspected the water, and got the addresses of the dead.
    draw dot_1 label_1 i_air t_air i_water t_water

    erase i_air t_air i_water t_water

Most had lived nearest one pump, in Broad Street. On 7 September he put the case to the parish board.
    draw dot_2 label_2 t_pump

    erase t_pump

The next day, the handle of the Broad Street pump came off.
    draw dot_3 label_3 t_handle

    erase t_handle

Only later did he draw each death as a short black bar on the street where it happened.
    draw dot_4 label_4 i_map t_map

## The map itself
zoom_from: dot_4
sources:
    - id: snow1855
      title: On the Mode of Communication of Cholera (2nd edition)
      authors: Snow J
      year: 1855
      finding: The Broad Street map; over 500 deaths in ten days; the brewery (about 70 workmen, none died), the workhouse (5 of 535) and the Hampstead widow.

    source snow_map cites ["snow1855"] x 290 y 430 width 440 of "Snow's cholera map, 1855" archive dr_map-1-cf-chefins-lith-london-to-accompany-on-the-mode-of-communicat-13187008 hidden true
    box brewery "Brewery: 0 of 70 died" cites ["snow1855"] x 805 y 520 width 300 height 70 font_size 28 hidden true
    box workhouse "Workhouse: 5 of 535" below brewery gap 50 width 300 height 70 font_size 28 hidden true
    box widow "Hampstead: she died" below workhouse gap 50 width 300 height 70 font_size 28 hidden true
    group cases members ["brewery","workhouse","widow"] walk true hidden true

Here is that map, from the 1855 edition of Snow's book on how cholera spreads.
    draw snow_map

    shape pump_ring #b5482e shape circle x 316 y 440 radius 30 style.stroke_width 4

Every black bar is one death, stacked at its address, and the stacks crowd round one point near the middle: the Broad Street pump.
    camera center.ref pump_ring zoom 1.4

But a cluster alone fits bad air too: the air near the pump was as foul as its water. What settled it were the exceptions.
    camera reset true

A brewery stood yards from the pump. Of its seventy or so workers, none died: they drank beer, and the brewery had its own well.
    draw brewery

The workhouse nearby held 535 people, and only five died. It had its own well too.
    draw workhouse

And a widow in Hampstead, miles away, died of cholera. She liked the taste of Broad Street water, and had it carried to her.
    draw widow

Same air, other water: spared. Other air, same water: dead. The exceptions turn a cluster into a cause.
    highlight cases

The story usually leaves out one thing: the outbreak was already fading when the handle came off. What the map did was make the argument, though it took years to win it.
    text t_fading "already fading" #b5482e x 805 y 190 font_size 32

    quiz "Which finding best shows it was the water, not the air?"
        right: They breathed their neighbours' air but drank other water. A cluster fits bad air too, and the outbreak was already fading.
        * Deaths crowded round the pump
        + The brewery men nearby were spared
        * Deaths fell after the handle came off

